  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Florida has $21.2B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date. There are 137,804 awards, with an average award size of $154K. Obligations are down 34.6% year over year, and current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is still in progress. Year-to-year trends show FY26 obligations at $21.2B versus FY25 at $32.5B, FY24 at $31.3B, FY23 at $29B, FY22 at $26.6B, and FY21 at $25.7B. The Department of Defense is by far the largest buyer with $14.5B in obligations, followed by agencies such as the Department of Veterans Affairs with $2.5B and the General Services Administration with $1.4B. Major vendors include LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $2.4B and $2.2B, THALLE CONSTRUCTION CO INC at $1.9B, and LOYAL SOURCE GOVERNMENT SERVICES LLC at $1.8B. Key industries include Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $4.4B and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $2.3B.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the drop in obligations compared with last year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 34.6% year over year. FY26 obligations are $21.2B compared with FY25 at $32.5B, so the current fiscal-year-to-date total is materially lower. The decline is visible across the statewide fiscal-year sequence: FY24 was $31.3B and FY23 was $29B. Current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is still in progress."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate federal contracting in Florida?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense dominates with $14.5B in obligations. Other top buying agencies are the Department of Veterans Affairs with $2.5B and the General Services Administration with $1.4B. Top vendors include LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $2.4B and $2.2B, THALLE CONSTRUCTION CO INC at $1.9B, and LOYAL SOURCE GOVERNMENT SERVICES LLC at $1.8B."
      }
    ]
